<p id=3D"passage-407"><a id=3D"chapter-03-section-17"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-408">3.17 A six-week editing apprenticeship</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-409">In week one, learn imports, the timeline, splits, tri=
ms, and export. Finish a simple sequence with no effects. In week two, impr=
ove caption accuracy and screen readability. Rebuild the same idea with one=
 purposeful crop and a clean text system. Keep the source footage similar s=
o that you can see the effect of the new decisions.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-410">In week three, focus on audio. Record a controlled co=
mparison, adjust levels, and test modest cleanup. In week four, focus on pa=
cing and continuity. Take a longer explanation and make two complete short =
videos, each with its own promise and evidence. Do not merely cut the recor=
ding at arbitrary time limits.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-411">In week five, test one AI-assisted operation against =
your manual process. Measure total time, including corrections and export r=
eview. In week six, build a reusable project template and produce a small s=
eries. Your template should reduce repetitive setup while allowing the stor=
y to vary.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-412">The schedule is a proposed practice framework, not a =
requirement to spend six weeks before publishing. You can publish suitable =
work throughout. Adjust the pace to your available time and current skills.=
 If a specific task remains confusing, repeat it with a new example rather =
than watching increasingly advanced tutorials that assume the foundation is=
 secure.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-413"><a id=3D"chapter-03-section-18"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-414">3.18 Judge AI by accepted minutes, not generated min=
utes</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-415">A tool that generates ten clips in a minute may still=
 require an hour of correction. Measure the time from source footage to an =
accepted export. Include transcript correction, crop repair, claim review, =
music replacement, and any work required because the tool chose an incomple=
te excerpt.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-416">Suppose manual editing takes ninety minutes and an AI=
 workflow takes forty minutes plus twenty minutes of cleanup. The time save=
d is thirty minutes, not fifty. If the supporting subscription costs thirty=
 dollars per month and you make four such videos, the direct tool cost is s=
even dollars and fifty cents per video before other expenses. These are hyp=
othetical planning numbers, not current vendor prices.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-417">Also track quality. Did the tool remove an essential =
limitation? Did it crop out the result? Did enhancement change the apparent=
 product sound in a microphone review? A faster process that weakens the ev=
idence can damage customer trust. Some tasks should remain manual because t=
he consequence of an error is significant relative to the time saved.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-418">Retain a simple acceptance rule: use automation when =
the reviewed output meets your standard and the complete workflow saves mea=
ningful time or enables a necessary capability. Reassess when the tool, you=
r footage, or your skill changes. A tool decision is part of an operating p=
rocess, not a permanent identity.</p>
