<p id=3D"passage-444"><a id=3D"chapter-04"></a></p>
<h1 id=3D"passage-445">4. Affiliate marketing and the TikTok Shop funnel</h=
1>
<p id=3D"passage-446">A viewer watches your demonstration and decides the p=
roduct might help. What happens next depends on the commercial model. They =
might tap a native product tag and buy inside TikTok. They might visit your=
 profile, open a comparison page, and follow an affiliate link to a softwar=
e vendor. They might buy your own ebook. These routes look similar from the=
 creator's camera, but they have different eligibility rules, economics, cu=
stomer relationships, and measurement limits.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-447">Affiliate marketing means earning compensation for a =
defined referral or qualifying action under an agreement. The merchant supp=
lies the product and normally controls checkout, fulfillment, support, and =
refunds. You supply useful discovery or evaluation. A commission is not the=
 same as the customer's full purchase amount, and a tracked order is not al=
ways a payable commission.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-448">This chapter teaches the affiliate model first, then =
works through native TikTok Shop, external affiliate links, product selecti=
on, demonstrations, and operations. It uses a United States baseline and so=
urces inspected on November 13, 2026. Platform access can differ by region,=
 account, product category, and pilot program. The examples are planning sc=
enarios, not claims that you or another beginner will earn a specified amou=
nt.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-449"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-1"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-450">4.1 Know which business you are operating</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-451">A creator affiliate recommends a merchant's product a=
nd receives a defined reward. A merchant affiliate program recruits creator=
s to sell the merchant's product. A service creator may be paid a fixed fee=
 to make a video regardless of referrals. A sponsored creator may receive p=
ayment for distribution or endorsement. One agreement can combine these arr=
angements, but each component should be explicit.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-452">For example, a microphone seller might offer you a sa=
mple and commission on qualifying purchases. A separate agreement might pay=
 for the video itself or for permission to use it in advertising. Receiving=
 a sample does not necessarily mean the merchant owes a production fee. Ear=
ning commission does not automatically give the merchant unlimited rights t=
o your face, footage, or advertising account.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-453">Your own digital product is a different business. If =
you sell an extension-building ebook, you control the product promise and d=
elivery. If other creators promote it, you become the merchant managing com=
missions and partner conduct. If you demonstrate another company's tool and=
 link to its subscription, you are the affiliate. Keeping these roles separ=
ate prevents confusing revenue reports and customer expectations.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-454">A good starting choice for your background is a small=
 number of developer or creator tools you genuinely use, alongside a narrow=
ly focused educational offer. That is a strategic hypothesis based on your =
strengths, not proof of existing demand. Broad physical-product promotion i=
s an optional experiment when the product is relevant to the same audience,=
 such as recording equipment used in your tutorials.</p>
