<p id=3D"passage-458">A marketing funnel describes how a group moves throug=
h stages: encountering content, understanding relevance, taking an action, =
evaluating an offer, purchasing, and receiving value. The term is a model, =
not a physical object or a requirement to build many pages. A native produc=
t tag followed by checkout can be a short funnel. An ebook sample followed =
by email lessons and a purchase can be a longer one.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-459">Each step should reduce a specific uncertainty. The v=
ideo establishes the problem and shows evidence. A product page clarifies c=
ompatibility, contents, price, and terms. Checkout enables payment. Deliver=
y provides the promised product. Support resolves problems. If a step adds =
effort without improving understanding or enabling the transaction, questio=
n whether it belongs.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-460">A native TikTok Shop route can be summarized as a sho=
ppable demonstration, a product listing, in-app checkout, and merchant fulf=
illment. An external software affiliate route may require a profile visit, =
a useful resource page, an approved affiliate link, the merchant's offer, a=
nd a qualifying purchase. The second route has more handoffs, but it can su=
pport more complex evaluation.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-461">Do not judge routes only by their number of steps. A =
technical subscription may require a longer demonstration to produce a well=
-informed customer who stays. Removing explanation can increase clicks whil=
e lowering fit. Your task is to reduce unnecessary friction while preservin=
g the information needed for an appropriate decision.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-462"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-3"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-463">4.3 Choose a product by fit and evidence</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-464">Begin with the viewer's task. If the audience is lear=
ning to make technical videos, a microphone or editing application may fit.=
 A random household item with a high commission may attract a different aud=
ience and weaken the channel's coherence. Relevance affects both communicat=
ion and long-term trust.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-465">Evaluate the product itself. Can you use it or inspec=
t reliable evidence? Can you demonstrate a meaningful benefit in a small fr=
ame? What are the limitations, compatibility requirements, ongoing costs, a=
nd alternatives? Does the merchant deliver and support it reliably? A high =
advertised commission cannot compensate for a product you cannot recommend =
truthfully.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-466">Then evaluate the program. Determine the qualifying e=
vent, attribution method, window, excluded customers, return or cancellatio=
n treatment, payment schedule, minimum payout, and permitted promotional me=
thods. Check whether the program allows email, paid advertising, trademark =
bidding, comparison pages, bonuses, and social links. Do not assume a pract=
ice is permitted because another creator appears to do it.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-467">Create an offer card with one row for each of these f=
acts and the date checked. If a fact is unknown, keep it unknown rather tha=
n filling it with an industry average. You can still draft a demonstration =
while investigating, but do not publish a compensation claim or build a pai=
d campaign around terms you have not verified.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-468"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-4"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-469">4.4 Calculate commission economics step by step</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-470">Suppose a hypothetical physical product costs forty d=
ollars and pays a fifteen-percent commission on the eligible amount. For a =
qualifying forty-dollar base, the gross commission is six dollars. If ten p=
ercent of tracked orders are later reversed under the program's rules, the =
expected retained commission per initially tracked order is five dollars an=
d forty cents. This is a simplified planning model, not a description of an=
y current program.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-471">Now suppose a video generates twenty thousand views. =
If one percent produce product-page visits, that is two hundred visits. If =
four percent of those visits become initially tracked orders, that is eight=
 orders. Eight times six dollars gives forty-eight dollars in gross tracked=
 commission. Applying the assumed reversal rate gives forty-three dollars a=
nd twenty cents in expected retained commission.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-472">If producing the video costs thirty dollars in direct=
 expenses and allocated tools, the expected contribution before your labor =
and other overhead is thirteen dollars and twenty cents. If you also value =
two hours of work at twenty dollars per hour for planning, the economic res=
ult becomes negative. The video may still be useful as a learning project o=
r reusable asset, but the initial commission figure is not profit.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-473">This arithmetic explains why views alone are insuffic=
ient. Product fit affects visit and purchase rates. Program terms affect el=
igible commission. Returns affect settlement. Production efficiency affects=
 cost. The companion's funnel experiment lets you vary these assumptions an=
d see where the result changes. Treat the output as sensitivity analysis, n=
ot a prediction engine.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-474"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-5"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-475">4.5 Compare two offers using expected value per rele=
vant visit</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-476">Imagine two hypothetical affiliate offers. Offer A pa=
ys six dollars per qualifying sale, converts four percent of relevant produ=
ct-page visits, and retains ninety percent of initially tracked commissions=
. Its expected retained commission per visit is six times 0.04 times 0.90, =
or twenty-one point six cents. Offer B pays fifteen dollars, converts one p=
ercent, and retains ninety-five percent. Its expected retained commission p=
er visit is fourteen point two five cents.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-477">The larger commission does not automatically produce =
the stronger result. These calculations still omit production cost, audienc=
e fit, attribution loss, payment timing, and customer outcomes. They are us=
eful because they make the relevant relationship visible. If you cannot est=
imate conversion with evidence, keep a range and test rather than selecting=
 the offer solely from the payout.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-478">Now consider a merchant changing its terms. If Offer =
A's commission drops to four dollars, expected retained commission per visi=
t becomes fourteen point four cents under the same other assumptions. A pre=
viously attractive offer can become marginal without any change in your vid=
eos. This is why you should review program terms periodically and avoid bui=
lding a fragile business around one payout figure.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-479">Do not confuse this visit-based quantity with revenue=
 per thousand video views. The latter also depends on how many viewers reac=
h the product page. A useful demonstration can improve the quality of those=
 visits while attracting fewer total viewers. Keep the stages separate so y=
ou can see whether a problem comes from the creative, the route, or the mer=
chant's conversion experience.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-480"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-6"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-481">4.6 Understand attribution and settlement</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-482">Attribution is the rule used to decide which referral=
 receives credit. A program may use a tracked link, a product tag, a code, =
or another approved mechanism. Cookies, device changes, competing referrals=
, browser restrictions, and purchase timing can affect recognition. The mer=
chant's system, not your expectation, determines the payable record under t=
he agreement.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-483">A pending commission represents a possible payment th=
at may depend on approval, return periods, cancellation, fraud review, or o=
ther conditions. A settled commission is closer to money you can rely on, t=
hough ordinary payment issues can still exist. Keep pending, approved, reve=
rsed, and paid amounts separate in your records.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-484">A coupon code can help some customers and measurement=
, but codes can be shared outside the intended audience. They can also inte=
ract with other attribution rules. Do not count every code use as a person =
who watched your video. Conversely, a viewer may see the video, search for =
the merchant later, and buy without creating a tracked referral. Your affil=
iate dashboard cannot observe every influence.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-485">Maintain a simple ledger of video identifiers, produc=
t identifiers, approved links, tracked clicks if available, pending orders,=
 reversals, settled commission, and payments received. Reconcile it periodi=
cally with the program dashboard. This gives you a better basis for decidin=
g what to produce than screenshots of a single day's pending revenue.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-486"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-7"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-487">4.7 Native TikTok Shop: verify the gates first</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-488">TikTok Shop is native commerce with its own seller, c=
reator, product, and content requirements. The inspected United States crea=
tor policy distinguishes affiliate creators, marketing creators bound to a =
shop, and official shop creators. It lists self-application and a minimum o=
f one thousand followers for affiliate creators, alongside age, location, i=
dentity, and account requirements. Bound-account roles have different rules=
 and permissions. Verify your actual in-app eligibility; meeting a follower=
 threshold alone does not guarantee approval. See <a href=3D"file:///C:/Use=
rs/amallick.ENGINEERS/Downloads/Short-Video-Commerce-Textbook.html#s13">S13=
</a>.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-489">A practical onboarding sequence is to confirm your co=
untry and age eligibility, inspect the creator-commerce application availab=
le to your account, complete required verification through the platform, an=
d review product access. Do not buy followers or treat a bound account as a=
 shortcut around requirements. Product-category access can also depend on c=
reator performance and account health.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-490">An affiliate creator promotes eligible merchant produ=
cts. An official shop account is tied to its own seller relationship. These=
 are not interchangeable. If your purpose is recommending other sellers' pr=
oducts, check the affiliate path. If your purpose is selling your own goods=
, check seller eligibility and the correct account relationship.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-491">Record what your account actually exposes. Generic tu=
torials may show another region, an older interface, or an account with dif=
ferent permissions. If a product-tag option is absent, investigate eligibil=
ity and product approval rather than adding a misleading graphic that looks=
 like a real tag. The route must function for the viewer, not merely resemb=
le a tutorial screenshot.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-492"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-8"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-493">4.8 Build the native product funnel</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-494">Choose one eligible product and inspect its listing. =
Confirm the exact variant you will demonstrate, price, shipping conditions,=
 compatibility, merchant identity, and return information available to buye=
rs. Order or obtain a legitimate sample when practical. Use the product eno=
ugh to identify an honest demonstration and a meaningful limitation.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-495">Create a video brief around a task: a desk recording =
with distracting echo, a phone stand that makes overhead filming possible, =
or a tool that organizes cables. Show the starting problem, demonstrate the=
 product under a fair condition, reveal the result, and state the relevant =
boundary. Add the approved product tag through the actual platform workflow=
 before publication.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-496">The call to action should point to the native destina=
tion you configured: inspect the tagged product and its current terms. Do n=
ot promise that the price or promotion will remain unchanged. A clean demon=
stration helps the viewer recognize whether the listing matches the product=
 shown. If the listing changes materially, reassess the content and link.</=
p>
<p id=3D"passage-497">After publication, monitor both content response and =
commerce outcomes available to your account. Questions about compatibility =
may suggest missing information. High clicks with poor purchases may indica=
te listing or offer problems. Purchases followed by complaints may indicate=
 product or expectation problems. The funnel continues through fulfillment;=
 a sale that disappoints the customer is not a successful recommendation.</=
p>
<p id=3D"passage-498"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-9"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-499">4.9 TikTok Shop and digital goods require a specific=
 distinction</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-500">The current United States prohibited-products policy =
does not support a blanket statement that all digital goods are always bann=
ed. It prohibits virtual or digital products unless approved under the Virt=
ual Goods category. The separate virtual-goods policy inspected for this gu=
ide makes that category invite-only and describes permitted types with addi=
tional conditions. This is a restricted route, not general permission to li=
st any ebook or subscription. See <a href=3D"file:///C:/Users/amallick.ENGI=
NEERS/Downloads/Short-Video-Commerce-Textbook.html#s14">S14</a>=E2=80=93<a =
href=3D"file:///C:/Users/amallick.ENGINEERS/Downloads/Short-Video-Commerce-=
Textbook.html#s15">S15</a>.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-501">For your initial ebook or SaaS, use an owned external=
 funnel unless your seller account and exact product have the required appr=
oval. A software category appearing in a policy does not establish that a r=
ecurring SaaS plan, a course, or your particular ebook is eligible. Confirm=
 the actual product type, approval, fulfillment method, and current seller =
rules before planning native commerce.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-502">Do not disguise a digital download as a shipped objec=
t or create a fake tracking number. Do not assume that connecting Shopify m=
akes a restricted product eligible. The store integration connects systems;=
 it does not override TikTok Shop's catalog rules. Your product and account=
 still need to qualify.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-503">This distinction changes the strategy. A physical-pro=
duct affiliate video can use a native tag when eligible. A digital teaching=
 product can use a permitted profile website route to your own page. An app=
roved virtual-goods seller may have another option, but that should be trea=
ted as an account-specific opportunity rather than the foundation of a begi=
nner's plan.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-504"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-10"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-505">4.10 External affiliate funnels from TikTok</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-506">For a software tool or an offer outside TikTok Shop, =
the viewer may need to visit your profile and follow a website link. The in=
spected account guidance states that personal accounts with at least one th=
ousand followers can post a website link. Business and regional eligibility=
 can differ; verify the actual field available to your account rather than =
assuming any account switch guarantees access. See <a href=3D"file:///C:/Us=
ers/amallick.ENGINEERS/Downloads/Short-Video-Commerce-Textbook.html#s16">S1=
6</a>.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-507">The simplest useful external route is a focused page =
that continues the video. A video about caption correction can lead to a ca=
ptioning workflow page with examples, limitations, and an appropriately dis=
closed affiliate link to the relevant tool. A generic page with twenty unre=
lated offers creates additional decisions and can weaken the relationship b=
etween the video and the product.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-508">Your page should add value beyond a redirect when the=
 program requires a substantive destination or the buyer needs comparison. =
Explain the task, show your own result, identify who the tool fits, and pro=
vide alternatives where helpful. Check the program's rules before using red=
irects, link shorteners, discounts, or paid traffic.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-509">If your account lacks a clickable website route, keep=
 building useful content and investigate legitimate eligibility. A memorabl=
e domain can be mentioned when appropriate, but it adds manual effort and i=
s not equivalent to a clickable link. Do not use spam comments or repeated =
unsolicited messages as a substitute. You can also prioritize YouTube's con=
figured profile route while the TikTok account develops.</p>
<p id=3D"passage-510"><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-11"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"passage-511">4.11 Disclose the relationship where it matters</h2>
<p id=3D"passage-512">For United States viewers, FTC guidance calls for mat=
erial relationships to be clear and hard to miss. Put an understandable dis=
